{"passages": [{"reference": "8.16", "text": "And so the humans associated with them then sought for the first time to do the same. Thus we are not born either good or evil; rather, we become so, and once habituated, we are hard to draw away. When the nonrational animals then ran out, the humans of mixed birth also tasted human flesh. For destroying their own flesh was no longer far removed from them, since they had previously tasted it in other forms.", "lexical_decisions": [{"source_quote": "οἱ δὲ οὖν αὐτοῖς ἄνθρωποι τὸ ὅμοιον τότε πρῶτον ποιεῖν ἐζήλωσαν", "chosen": "the humans associated with them then sought for the first time to do the same", "rationale": "The main rendering takes αὐτοῖς with ἄνθρωποι, marking the humans’ association with the previously mentioned beings. Alternatively, αὐτοῖς may identify the model of τὸ ὅμοιον: “And so humans then first sought to do the same as they did.” The associative reading is retained because of the placement of αὐτοῖς beside ἄνθρωποι, but the comparative attachment is also viable."}, {"source_quote": "ἐθισθέντες δυσαποσπάστως ἔχομεν", "chosen": "once habituated, we are hard to draw away", "rationale": "The expression depicts established habits as difficult to break; more literally, “having become accustomed, we are in a state difficult to detach.”"}, {"source_quote": "οἱ νόθοι ἄνθρωποι", "chosen": "the humans of mixed birth", "rationale": "νόθοι denotes illegitimate or mixed parentage here, referring to the hybrid origin described in the surrounding narrative; “bastard humans” would be more blunt."}, {"source_quote": "τὴν ἰδίαν διαφθεῖραι σάρκα", "chosen": "destroying their own flesh", "rationale": "The wording preserves the source’s image. In context, “their own flesh” likely means human flesh belonging to their own kind rather than their individual bodies."}, {"source_quote": "ἐν ἑτέραις μορφαῖς αὐτῆς γευσαμένοις", "chosen": "since they had previously tasted it in other forms", "rationale": "The pronoun refers back to flesh, presenting animal flesh as that same flesh under different forms; the unusual conceptual identification is retained."}], "source_issues": []}, {"reference": "8.17", "text": "Because of the great outpouring of blood, the pure air was defiled by an impure exhalation and became diseased, making those who breathed it sickly, so that thereafter people died prematurely. The earth, being severely defiled by these things, then for the first time spewed out venomous and destructive creatures. Since everything had consequently moved toward the worse because of the beastlike demons, God planned to remove them like bad leaven, lest, from evil seed, each successive generation should become like the one before it, act impiously in the same way, and empty the coming age of men who would be saved. For precisely this reason, after announcing beforehand to one righteous man that he was to be preserved in a chest with the three born from him, together with their wives and all the rest, God sent water flooding in as a deluge, so that, once everything had been destroyed, the world, thoroughly purged, might be returned clean to the one preserved in the chest for a second beginning of life. And indeed, so it happened.", "lexical_decisions": [{"source_quote": "ἀκαθάρτῳ ἀναθυμιάσει", "chosen": "by an impure exhalation", "rationale": "ἀναθυμίασις denotes vapor or exhalation; “impure” preserves the contrast with the previously “pure” air."}, {"source_quote": "ἀώρους ἀποθνήσκειν", "chosen": "died prematurely", "rationale": "ἄωρος describes death occurring before its proper time; “died young” is possible but more narrowly age-specific."}, {"source_quote": "τὰ ἰοβόλα καὶ λυμαντικὰ ζῷα", "chosen": "venomous and destructive creatures", "rationale": "ἰοβόλα literally suggests creatures that cast or inject venom, while λυμαντικά describes those that ravage or cause harm."}, {"source_quote": "σωζομένων ἀνδρῶν", "chosen": "men who would be saved", "rationale": "The participle can denote men receiving preservation or salvation. “Surviving men” is possible in the flood context, but the coming-age language supports the broader rendering."}, {"source_quote": "ἐν λάρνακι", "chosen": "in a chest", "rationale": "λάρναξ is a chest, coffer, or container and is not the usual Septuagint term for Noah’s ark. “In an ark” is contextually possible but would obscure the source’s distinct word choice."}, {"source_quote": "ἅμα τοῖς λοιποῖς", "chosen": "together with all the rest", "rationale": "The source leaves the referent of “the rest” unstated, likely the other beings preserved in the flood vessel; the translation does not supply an explicit noun."}], "source_issues": []}, {"reference": "8.18", "text": "Since the souls of the dead giants were larger than human souls—since the giants also surpassed humans in bodily size—they were designated, as a new race, by a new name as well. For these newcomers to the world, a law prescribing how they must live was laid down by God through an angel. For since they were hybrid in origin, from the fire of angels and the blood of women, and for that reason were destined to desire a certain race of their own, they were forestalled by a certain just law. For an angel was sent out to them by God, disclosing his will and saying:", "lexical_decisions": [{"source_quote": "αἱ τῶν τεθνεώτων γιγάντων ψυχαὶ τῶν ἀνθρωπείων ψυχῶν μείζους ἦσαν", "chosen": "the souls of the dead giants were larger than human souls", "rationale": "μείζους may mean “greater” or “larger”; the following appeal to bodily size favors the spatial sense while retaining the author’s unusual claim about souls."}, {"source_quote": "ἅτε δὴ καὶ τοῖς σώμασιν ὑπερέχουσαι", "chosen": "since the giants also surpassed humans in bodily size", "rationale": "Grammatically the feminine participle refers to the souls, yielding the more literal but difficult “since they also surpassed them with respect to bodies.” The contextual rendering understands the bodily comparison as one between giants and humans."}, {"source_quote": "ὡς καινὸν γένος καινῷ καὶ τῷ ὀνόματι προσηγορεύθησαν", "chosen": "they were designated, as a new race, by a new name as well", "rationale": "προσηγορεύθησαν concerns being called or designated; the source does not state the new name in this clause."}, {"source_quote": "οἷς ἐπηλύτοις τῷ κόσμῳ", "chosen": "for these newcomers to the world", "rationale": "ἐπήλυτος denotes one who has come from elsewhere, hence an alien, immigrant, or newcomer; “newcomers” best fits their newly arising race."}, {"source_quote": "ἰδίου τινὸς γένους ἐπιθυμεῖν μέλλοντες", "chosen": "were destined to desire a certain race of their own", "rationale": "γένος can mean race, kind, or class. The object of their anticipated desire remains unusual and underspecified, so the translation retains “race” rather than supplying what that desire entails."}, {"source_quote": "δικαίῳ τινὶ προελήφθησαν νόμῳ", "chosen": "they were forestalled by a certain just law", "rationale": "προλαμβάνω here presents the law as acting beforehand to anticipate or restrain their intended conduct; “were anticipated by” is more literal but less clear."}], "source_issues": []}, {"reference": "8.19", "text": "“The following seems good to the all-seeing God: you are to rule over no human being and harass no one, unless someone enslaves himself to you by worshiping you, sacrificing, pouring libations, partaking of your table, performing some other thing that ought not to be done, shedding blood, tasting dead flesh, or filling himself with what a wild animal has left, with something cut up, with something strangled, or with some other unclean thing. But those taking refuge in my law you will not only refrain from touching; you will also give them honor and flee from their presence. Whatever seems right to them against you, since they are righteous, that you will have to suffer. If some of those devoted to me sin—whether by committing adultery, practicing magic, living impurely, or doing some other thing that does not please me—then at my command they will have to suffer something at your hands or at the hands of others. In their case too, when they reorient themselves, I will judge their reorientation—whether it is worthy of forgiveness or not—and render the decision. You therefore must remember and do these things, knowing well that not even your thoughts will be able to escape his notice.”", "lexical_decisions": [{"source_quote": "μηδενὸς ἀνθρώπων ὑμᾶς κυριεύειν", "chosen": "you are to rule over no human being", "rationale": "κυριεύειν denotes exercising mastery or lordship over someone; “rule over” retains the authority claim without importing a divine title."}, {"source_quote": "ἑαυτὸν ὑμῖν καταδουλώσῃ", "chosen": "enslaves himself to you", "rationale": "The compound verb expresses reducing oneself to servitude or slavery, stronger than merely “submitting.”"}, {"source_quote": "ἢ θηρίου λειψάνου ἢ τμητοῦ ἢ πνικτοῦ", "chosen": "with what a wild animal has left, with something cut up, with something strangled", "rationale": "The genitives depend on “filling himself.” θηρίου λείψανον can alternatively mean “the remains of a wild animal,” while τμητόν may be “something cut off” rather than generally “cut up.”"}, {"source_quote": "ὅ,τι γὰρ ἂν αὐτοῖς δικαίοις οὖσι καθ’ ὑμῶν δοκῇ", "chosen": "Whatever seems right to them against you, since they are righteous", "rationale": "δοκῇ can mean what seems good, right, or fitting to them. “Whatever judgment they make against you” is possible but makes the judicial sense more explicit than the Greek does."}, {"source_quote": "τῶν ἐμοὶ προσκειμένων", "chosen": "those devoted to me", "rationale": "πρόσκειμαι can describe attachment, adherence, or devotion; “those belonging to me” is possible but more strongly possessive."}, {"source_quote": "οἷς καὶ μετανοήσασιν, τὴν μετάνοιαν κρίνας", "chosen": "In their case too, when they reorient themselves, I will judge their reorientation", "rationale": "The participle describes moral reconsideration after wrongdoing. “When they repent, I will judge their repentance” is the traditional alternative; the destination of the reorientation is not added because the clause does not state it."}, {"source_quote": "οὐδὲ αἱ ἐνθυμήσεις ὑμῶν λαθεῖν αὐτὸν δυνήσονται", "chosen": "not even your thoughts will be able to escape his notice", "rationale": "The third-person αὐτόν refers to God and is retained despite the preceding first-person divine speech, preserving the source’s shift in person."}], "source_issues": [{"source_quote": "ἢ· μοιχευσάμενοι", "issue": "The printed sequence contains an anomalous raised stop immediately after the disjunctive ἢ. The surrounding series supports taking μοιχευσάμενοι as the first item in the list without a syntactic break.", "proposed_reading": "ἢ μοιχευσάμενοι", "blocks_publication": false}, {"source_quote": "τότε-ἐξ ἐμῆς κελεύσεως", "issue": "The printed hyphen between τότε and ἐξ has no evident syntactic function. The words yield a clear construction when read with ordinary separation, “then at my command.”", "proposed_reading": "τότε ἐξ ἐμῆς κελεύσεως", "blocks_publication": false}]}, {"reference": "8.20", "text": "After giving them such instructions, the angel departed. But you still do not know this law: anyone who worships demons, sacrifices, or shares their table becomes enslaved to them and, as someone under evil masters, will share in every punishment that comes from them. Because of this ignorance, you have been corrupted at their altars and filled with them; you have come under their authority, and, being abused in every way, you are sick in your bodies. But you must know that the demons have authority over no one unless a person first shares their way of life, seeing that not even their ruler can do anything contrary to the law laid down against them by God. Therefore he has no authority over anyone who has not worshiped him. Nor can anyone receive from them anything he wants; neither can he be harmed at all, as you can learn from there.", "lexical_decisions": [{"source_quote": "ὑπόδουλος αὐτοῖς γενόμενος", "chosen": "becomes enslaved to them", "rationale": "ὑπόδουλος describes being subject as a slave, not merely being influenced or loosely subordinate."}, {"source_quote": "πάσης τῆς ἐξ αὐτῶν μεθέξει τιμωρίας", "chosen": "will share in every punishment that comes from them", "rationale": "ἐξ αὐτῶν can indicate punishment proceeding from or inflicted by the demons. “Their entire punishment” is grammatically possible but less consistent with the comparison to evil masters."}, {"source_quote": "τοῖς αὐτῶν βωμοῖς προσφθαρέντες", "chosen": "you have been corrupted at their altars", "rationale": "The passive participle denotes being corrupted or ruined in connection with the altars. “Seduced toward their altars” is a possible contextual nuance."}, {"source_quote": "ἐὰν μὴ πρότερόν τις αὐτοῖς ὁμοδίαιτος γένηται", "chosen": "unless a person first shares their way of life", "rationale": "ὁμοδίαιτος denotes one who shares another’s manner of life or board. “Becomes their table-companion” would emphasize the sacrificial meals mentioned earlier."}, {"source_quote": "ὁπότε οὐδὲ ὁ αὐτῶν ἄρχων", "chosen": "seeing that not even their ruler", "rationale": "ὁπότε can be temporal, “when,” but here introduces the supporting circumstance that even the demons’ ruler is bound by the law."}, {"source_quote": "ὡς ἐκεῖθεν δύνασθε μαθεῖν", "chosen": "as you can learn from there", "rationale": "ἐκεῖθεν is deictic and literally means “from there.” It may point to an ensuing illustration, in which case “from what follows” would be a possible contextual rendering."}], "source_issues": []}]}
